  { id:'opmcq-c8-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 70-year-old woman presents with acute redness and severe ocular pain in her right eye. She reports tingling over the right forehead for 2 days. Vision is reduced to 6/18. Slit-lamp exam shows conjunctival injection, fine keratic precipitates, and pseudodendritic corneal epithelial lesions that stain poorly with fluorescein and lack terminal bulbs. Corneal sensation is markedly reduced. Intraocular pressure is elevated at 32 mmHg. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Herpes simplex epithelial keratitis","HZO (Herpes zoster ophthalmicus)","CMV anterior uveitis","Toxic epitheliopathy"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem is the deck's HZO slide read out as a case, and the discriminator is printed there as an explicit contrast.** `L15,16) Cornea` slide \"Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus (HZO)\" prints:\n\n> Corneal manifestations: punctate epithelial keratitis, **pseudodendritic ulcer**, mucous plaques, disciform keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy\n>\n> Note: **HZO pseudodendrite is smaller, stellate, LACKS TERMINAL BUDS** (cf. HSV dendrite)\n\nand its complication box lists **neurotrophic keratopathy**, with the closing line \"Long-term: post-herpetic neuralgia, **corneal anaesthesia** (neurotrophic keratopathy)\". Three findings in the stem are that slide verbatim — a pseudodendrite, no terminal bulbs, and markedly reduced corneal sensation — and the prodromal tingling over the V1 dermatome is the fourth.\n\n| Finding in the stem | What it excludes |\n|---|---|\n| Tingling over the forehead for 2 days | A dermatomal prodrome in CN V1. Nothing else on the menu has one |\n| **Pseudodendritic** lesion **lacking terminal bulbs** | HSV. The deck's HSV slide makes the dendrite's \"linear branching pattern **with terminal buds**\" pathognomonic |\n| **Stains poorly** with fluorescein | HSV again — \"Fluorescein: **stains the ulcer bed**\" is the HSV epithelial finding, because there is a true epithelial defect. The zoster pseudodendrite is a raised mucous plaque, not an excavated ulcer |\n| Corneal sensation **markedly reduced** | Sits with zoster's neurotrophic keratopathy. ⚠️ Note it does *not* exclude HSV, which also anaesthetises the cornea — the deck lists \"HSV, HZV\" together as causes of neurotrophic keratopathy |\n| Fine keratic precipitates + IOP 32 | Anterior segment inflammation with raised pressure |\n\n**The pressure of 32 mmHg is the one finding the cornea deck cannot explain, and the book can.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 gives the mechanism under anterior uveitis: IOP usually *falls* in iritis from ciliary-body shutdown, but \"sometimes the IOP increases [Secondary inflammatory glaucoma] due to: • **Trabeculitis as in HSV or HZV iritis** • Plasmoid aqueous and circulating large-sized WBCs obstruct the trabecular meshwork\". A hypertensive anterior uveitis is a herpetic signature, and it points at both herpesviruses rather than at either one.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Herpes simplex epithelial keratitis** | ⚠️ **The designed trap, and it fails on exactly two words.** Everything except *pseudo*dendritic and *lacks terminal bulbs* fits HSV — recurrent epithelial disease, reduced sensation, trabeculitis. The bank withheld the age-and-dermatome story and then handed over the two morphological facts that separate the pair |\n| **B. HZO** | The key |\n| **C. CMV anterior uveitis** | CMV is named in this course only as one item in a list of viral causes of uveitis — `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 11 (\"Viral: e.g.; HSV, HZV, CMV,….\") and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134 (\"HSV, HZV, CMV especially with acquired immunedeficiency\"). Neither source gives CMV an epithelial corneal lesion of any kind, and CMV anterior uveitis is a disease of the immunocompromised, typically without a dermatomal prodrome *(the last clause is **not taken from the course material** — the course prints CMV only as a list item)* |\n| **D. Toxic epitheliopathy** | Drug-induced surface toxicity. The chapter teaches the concept only through topical-anaesthetic abuse (\"Inhibit epithelial healing · Cause stromal melting · Anaesthetic keratopathy → perforation\"). No drug history is given, and toxic epitheliopathy is diffuse punctate staining, not a branching figure |\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain works the same disease from the skin inwards — `opqb-t8-324` gives vesicular eruptions of the forehead, nose and scalp with neuralgic pain and keys \"Typical corneal dendrites may occur\", and `opqb-t8-343` asks which sign predicts corneal involvement (eruption on the tip of the nose). Neither is this question: both begin from a visible rash, and this stem never mentions one.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A V1 prodrome with a pseudodendrite that lacks terminal bulbs, stains poorly, and sits on an anaesthetic cornea is herpes zoster ophthalmicus, not herpes simplex; the raised IOP is herpetic trabeculitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.55 (Ch.8 Q1)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 38-year-old male patient complains of eye pain, excessive tearing and redness. History is significant for severe unilateral facial pain that was followed by appearance of skin vesicles. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Herpes simplex keratitis","Herpes zoster ophthalmicus","Calcific band keratopathy","Exposure keratopathy","Geronotoxon"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Two words carry the whole question: unilateral and vesicles.** `L15,16) Cornea` slide \"Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus (HZO)\" opens \"Varicella-zoster virus reactivation in **CN V1 (ophthalmic) dermatome**\" — a reactivation in one dermatome of one nerve, which is why zoster pain and zoster rash are unilateral and why the pain comes *first*. Severe neuralgic pain preceding the eruption by days is the classic sequence.\n\n| Option | Why it is or is not the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Herpes simplex keratitis** | HSV does produce lid vesicles, and the deck names \"Herpes Simplex / Zoster\" side by side in its own outline. What it does not produce is **severe unilateral facial pain preceding** the rash. HSV epithelial disease is described by the deck through the corneal lesion — dendrite with terminal buds — and its scenario slide gives \"mild eye pain, photophobia, and redness\", not facial neuralgia |\n| **B. Herpes zoster ophthalmicus** | The key |\n| **C. Calcific band keratopathy** | Calcium in Bowman's layer in the interpalpebral zone, from chronic uveitis, hypercalcaemia, silicone oil, gout. Painless, gradual, no rash. This chapter asks it directly twice more — `opmcq-c8-214` and `opmcq-c8-28` |\n| **D. Exposure keratopathy** | \"Incomplete lid closure → inferior epithelial breakdown\", from VII nerve palsy, thyroid eye disease or proptosis. ⚠️ Worth pausing over, because a **VII** palsy is also a cranial-nerve lesion that can follow herpes zoster (Ramsay Hunt). But exposure keratopathy is defined by the lid failing to close, which the stem never mentions, and it produces no vesicles |\n| **E. Geronotoxon** | Printed exactly so. **Gerontoxon is a synonym for arcus senilis** — the lipid arc at the limbus. Painless, bilateral, age-related, no rash. The chapter's own arcus questions are `opmcq-c8-20` and `opmcq-c8-21`. *(The synonymy is **not taken from the course material**: `L15,16) Cornea` names the entity only as \"Arcus senilis\" and the word gerontoxon appears nowhere in the module's twenty-nine cached decks or twelve cached book chapters.)* |\n\n**A is the only real competitor, and the discrimination is the order of events.** Pain then vesicles is zoster; the herpes-simplex story in this course starts at the cornea and never starts in a dermatome.\n\n⚠️ **This is the antecedent of `opmcq-c8-3` and `opmcq-c8-4`**, both of which are printed as \"In the previous case…\" and neither of which names its patient. Read the three together.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Severe unilateral facial pain followed by skin vesicles, with a red painful watering eye, is herpes zoster ophthalmicus — VZV reactivating in the V1 dermatome, pain before rash.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.55 (Ch.8 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 38-year-old man had severe unilateral facial pain followed by an eruption of skin vesicles, and now has eye pain, excessive tearing and redness. Which of the following complications can occur?",
    options:["Neuralgic pain","Lid ptosis, scarring, ectropion and entropion","Scleral and limbal atrophy","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following complications can occur?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c8-2`, printed immediately above it on book p.55, and it was read rather than assumed. The restatement above repeats that patient's **findings** and stops there: the word *zoster* is deliberately withheld, because naming the diagnosis would answer `opmcq-c8-2`.\n\n**Two of the three limbs are printed in the deck's own complication box.** `L15,16) Cornea` slide \"Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus (HZO)\" boxes the complications as: epithelial keratitis · stromal / disciform keratitis · neurotrophic keratopathy · **scleritis / episcleritis** · **post-herpetic neuralgia**, and closes \"Long-term: post-herpetic neuralgia, corneal anaesthesia\".\n\n| Option | Where the course puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Neuralgic pain** | **Post-herpetic neuralgia**, printed twice on the HZO slide — once in the complication box and once in the long-term line. `opmcq-c8-12` keys it as the *most* important and prevalent complication and `opmcq-c8-13` asks its treatment |\n| **B. Lid ptosis, scarring, ectropion and entropion** | Cicatricial lid disease following the healing of the vesicular eruption. ⚠️ **The cornea deck does not print this**, and neither does `L5) Eyelid disease`, whose own not-covered list records \"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus, Hutchinson's sign, eyelid herpes simplex — ABSENT\" and notes that slide 36 **photographs** nose-tip zoster without naming the sign. `book/ch05-eyelids` records the same absence. The mechanism is nevertheless standard: deep dermal necrosis in the V1 dermatome scars, and a scarred lid contracts into cicatricial ectropion or, with tarsal involvement, entropion, while levator or aponeurotic involvement gives ptosis. **Not taken from the course material** — the course teaches cicatricial ectropion from \"lacerated wounds of the eyelids\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.78) but never links it to zoster |\n| **C. Scleral and limbal atrophy** | The deck lists **scleritis / episcleritis** among HZO complications, and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` teaches that necrotizing scleritis thins the sclera. Scleral atrophy is the healed end of that. **Limbal** atrophy — stem-cell loss at the limbus after herpetic disease — is **not taken from the course material**; no cached deck or book chapter uses the phrase |\n| **D. All of the above** | The key |\n\n**Why \"All of the above\" is right even though only one limb is printed cleanly.** Each of the three is a genuine complication of the same disease, and the question asks what *can* occur, not what is commonest. A reader who takes A alone is answering `opmcq-c8-12`, which is the question that asks for the most important one.\n\n⚠️ **The gap here is real and it is filled rather than declared.** Measured: `entropion` returns hits in eleven cached files and **not one of them is in a herpes-zoster context** — they are trachoma, trauma, lid disease and the red-eye chapter. `L15,16) Cornea` mentions the eyelid only through exposure keratopathy. So the lid-scarring limb of this question is answerable from the bank's own key and from general medical knowledge, not from these sources.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus can leave post-herpetic neuralgia, cicatricial lid disease (ptosis, scarring, ectropion, entropion) and scleral or limbal atrophy — the deck prints the neuralgia and the scleritis, the lid sequelae are general knowledge.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.55 (Ch.8 Q3; printed "In the previous case, which of the following complications can occur?" and refers back to Ch.8 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 38-year-old man had severe unilateral facial pain followed by an eruption of skin vesicles, and now has eye pain, excessive tearing and redness. Which of the following is the line of management used to properly treat that condition?",
    options:["Topical ganciclovir","Oral acyclovir","Systemic steroids","Surgical intervention"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following is the line of management use to properly treat that condition?\"** (\"use\" for \"used\" is the bank's). The back-reference is **two questions deep**: `opmcq-c8-3` is itself a back-reference and introduces no new patient, so \"the previous case\" is still `opmcq-c8-2`'s man. Both were read. The restatement withholds both the diagnosis (which is `opmcq-c8-2`'s key) and the complication list (which is `opmcq-c8-3`'s).\n\n**The deck prints a systemic antiviral and prints it with a deadline.** `L15,16) Cornea`, HZO slide:\n\n> Treatment: **oral valaciclovir/famciclovir within 72 h of rash onset**\n\nValaciclovir is the valine ester prodrug of aciclovir, and aciclovir itself is the drug the same deck prescribes orally elsewhere — \"Prophylactic **oral aciclovir 400 mg twice daily** reduces recurrence rate by ~50%\". Oral acyclovir is therefore the option on this menu that matches the printed class, the printed route and the printed intent. ⚠️ *That valaciclovir and famciclovir are prodrugs/analogues of aciclovir is **not taken from the course material** — the deck names the three drugs without relating them.*\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Topical ganciclovir** | ⚠️ **The strongest wrong answer, and it is right for the other herpesvirus.** The deck's dendritic-ulcer scenario keys \"Topical antiviral (aciclovir 3% ointment 5x/day **or ganciclovir gel**)\" — for **HSV epithelial** keratitis. This chapter uses that fact as `opmcq-c8-23`'s key. Zoster is a systemic dermatomal infection; a corneal gel does not treat a dermatome, and the deck never offers a topical agent for HZO |\n| **B. Oral acyclovir** | The key |\n| **C. Systemic steroids** | Steroids appear in this deck for HSV **disciform** (stromal immune) keratitis and for graft rejection, always with antiviral cover, and the deck's flagged prescribing error is \"Topical Steroids in Epithelial HSV\". Steroid monotherapy in acute zoster treats neither the virus nor the printed target |\n| **D. Surgical intervention** | Nothing in the HZO block is surgical. Surgery enters the cornea deck for keratoplasty, tarsorrhaphy and refractive procedures |\n\n**The 72-hour window is the part worth carrying.** The printed indication is not \"zoster\" but \"zoster **within 72 h of rash onset**\" — the antiviral works while the virus is still replicating, which is also why it reduces, rather than abolishes, post-herpetic neuralgia.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus is treated with a systemic antiviral started within 72 hours of the rash — oral aciclovir or its prodrugs valaciclovir and famciclovir; topical ganciclovir belongs to HSV epithelial keratitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.55 (Ch.8 Q4; printed "In the previous case, which of the following is the line of management use to properly treat that condition?" and refers back through Ch.8 Q3 to Ch.8 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following is among the complications of herpes zoster ophthalmicus?",
    options:["Dacryocystitis","Entropion","Trigeminal nerve palsy","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION IS MISPRINTED AND THE MISPRINT IS RECORDED, NOT CORRECTED.** `ophthalmology MCQ.pdf` p.56 prints **five** option lines and uses the letter **B twice, with identical text**:\n\n> A. Dacryocystitis\n> **B. Entropion**\n> **B. Entropion**\n> C. Trigeminal nerve palsy\n> D. None of the above\n\nVerified at 400 dpi — a genuine duplicated line, not a scan artefact. Because both duplicates carry the same letter *and* the same text, the printed key **B** is unambiguous, and the entry ships the four distinct options in printed order. The staging record `house-c8.array.js` keeps all five lines.\n\n**Entropion is the answer by elimination, and the elimination is clean.**\n\n| Option | The course's position |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Dacryocystitis** | Infection of the lacrimal sac. `L9) lacrimal system.` and `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.4 give its causes as nasolacrimal duct obstruction with stasis — **herpes zoster is not among them**, and the cornea deck's HZO complication box does not mention the lacrimal system. ⚠️ Measured: `dacryocystitis` returns hits in nine cached files and **none of them is a zoster context** |\n| **B. Entropion** | ⚠️ The key. Not printed in `L15,16) Cornea` either — the HZO box lists epithelial keratitis, stromal/disciform keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy, scleritis/episcleritis and post-herpetic neuralgia, and stops. The link is the lid scarring of the healed dermatomal eruption: cicatrisation of the tarsus and conjunctival surface rotates the margin inward. **Not taken from the course material.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.78 does teach cicatricial lid malposition, but from lacerated wounds, never from zoster. Consistent with this chapter's own `opmcq-c8-3`, which keys the same sequelae |\n| **C. Trigeminal nerve palsy** | ⚠️ **The most interesting distractor, because it is nearly true and fails on one word.** HZO *is* a disease of the trigeminal nerve — V1 specifically — and it *does* damage it: the deck's own \"corneal anaesthesia (neurotrophic keratopathy)\" is trigeminal **sensory** loss, and `opmcq-c8-1`'s markedly reduced corneal sensation is that damage in a patient. But *palsy* means motor weakness, and the trigeminal's motor supply is to the muscles of mastication through **V3** — a division zoster ophthalmicus does not involve. A trigeminal neuropathy is right; a trigeminal palsy is not. *(The V1-sensory / V3-motor split is **not taken from the course material**: `trigeminal` returns three cached files, and all three treat it as the cornea's sensory supply)* |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails because B is a real sequela |\n\n**How to hold this one.** Zoster damages the trigeminal nerve's *sensation* and the eyelid's *shape*. The option that says \"palsy\" is describing motor loss the disease does not cause; the option that says \"entropion\" is describing scarring it does.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Cicatricial entropion follows the healed lid eruption of herpes zoster ophthalmicus; zoster causes trigeminal sensory loss (corneal anaesthesia), not a trigeminal motor palsy, and has no link to dacryocystitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.56 (Ch.8 Q5; ⚠️ printed with FIVE option lines, the letter B used twice with the identical text "Entropion" — the duplicate is collapsed here and recorded in house-c8.array.js)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:"A young boy with Down syndrome presents to the ophthalmologist with conical protrusion and thinning of the cornea. Which of the following is a useful tool for decision making and management of that patient?",
    options:["Amsler's grid","CT orbit","Pentacam","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The stem is keratoconus written as a definition plus its printed risk factor.** `L15,16) Cornea` slide \"Keratoconus: Diagnosis & Management\":\n\n> Progressive bilateral (often asymmetric) corneal **thinning and protrusion** … Risk factors: eye rubbing, atopy, **Down syndrome**, connective tissue disorders\n\nand the slide before it defines ectasia as \"progressive corneal **thinning & protrusion**\". Conical protrusion + thinning + Down syndrome is the diagnosis handed over three times.\n\n**What the course says the decision tool is.** The same slide ends \"**Topography**: inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie pattern\", the ectasia slide states \"Screening for ectasia is **MANDATORY** before refractive surgery (corneal topography)\", and the refractive-surgery slide lists the pre-op must-checks as \"Corneal **topography** – RULE OUT ectasia\" and \"**Pachymetry** – minimum thickness\". Corneal imaging — curvature and thickness — is what decides whether this eye gets spectacles, lenses, cross-linking, ring segments or a graft.\n\n⚠️ **THE WORD \"PENTACAM\" IS NOT IN THIS COURSE, AND THE GAP IS FILLED RATHER THAN DECLARED.** Measured: `pentacam` returns **exactly zero hits across all twenty-nine cached lecture files and all twelve cached book chapters**, case-insensitively. The Pentacam is a rotating-Scheimpflug tomographer: it photographs the cornea through 360° and reconstructs anterior **and posterior** elevation maps plus a full pachymetry map, so a single scan delivers everything the deck asks for by name — topography, pachymetry, and posterior-surface change, which is the earliest ectatic sign. It is the instrument the deck describes without naming. **Not taken from the course material.**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Amsler's grid** | Printed \"Amsler's gird\" in the bank. A **macular** function test — a squared chart read at reading distance for metamorphopsia and scotoma. It is taught in this module under the fundus and the optic nerve (`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…`, `L21) Optic Nerve`, `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway`, `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination`, `book/ch01-examination`, `book/ch14-neuro-ophthalmology`) and **never once in a corneal context**. It says nothing about corneal shape |\n| **B. CT orbit** | Cross-sectional imaging of the bony orbit and its contents — the cornea deck's only CT indication is \"check for retained **intraocular foreign body** (CT scan)\". CT resolves millimetres; keratoconus is measured in microns of thickness and dioptres of curvature |\n| **C. Pentacam** | The key |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n**Filed in `op-cornea-surg`** — *\"Cornea — ectasia, grafts and refractive surgery\"* — because keratoconus is ectasia, which is that chapter's first word.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain asks the same instrument question in the course's own vocabulary. `opqb-t8-341` keys **computerized topography** as the earliest indicator of keratoconus and `opqb-t8-316` keys \"Topography & pachymetry\" as the pre-LASIK work-up. Same decision, named the way the deck names it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Conical corneal protrusion with thinning in a child with Down syndrome is keratoconus, and management is decided by corneal imaging — topography plus pachymetry, delivered together by Scheimpflug tomography; the Amsler grid is a macular test and CT resolves nothing corneal.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.56 (Ch.8 Q6; option A printed "Amsler\'s gird")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following symptoms will be present in a patient with corneal foreign body?",
    options:["Pain","Excessive lacrimation","Red eye","Photophobia","All of the above"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**A corneal foreign body breaks the epithelium, and the course explains every one of these four from that single fact.**\n\n`L37) Red Eye`, which is a book chapter rather than a slide deck, opens its corneal section with the mechanism:\n\n> **Disruption of the corneal epithelium results in significant pain for the patient.** This disruption can occur due to trauma, causing a corneal abrasion or **foreign body injury**, or due to bacterial or viral infections.\n\nThat is the pain. The cornea is \"the **most densely innervated tissue in the body** (trigeminal nerve, ophthalmic division)\" (`L15,16) Cornea`, anatomy overview), which is why a speck of metal on it hurts more than a laceration elsewhere on the body — and the same trigeminal afferents drive the reflex tearing and the lid spasm.\n\n**The other three are in the same chapter's Table 4-1, *Symptoms of a Red Eye*.** Reading the **keratitis** column — which is where a foreign-body epithelial defect sits — on the table's 0–3 severity scale:\n\n| Symptom | Keratitis score | Referral advisable? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Blurred vision | **3** | Yes |\n| Pain | **2** | Yes |\n| **Photophobia** | **3** | Yes |\n| Coloured halos | 0 | Yes |\n| Exudation | 0–3 | No |\n| Itching | 0 | No |\n\nPhotophobia scores maximum. `L37` explains it separately: \"Photophobia is an abnormal sensitivity to light that accompanies iridocyclitis, either alone or **secondary to corneal inflammation**.\" And the same chapter's entropion paragraph — a lash mechanically rubbing the cornea, which is a foreign body in all but name — states the quartet outright: \"can result in **pain, photophobia, tearing, and redness** of the eye\".\n\n| Option | Present? |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Pain** | Yes — `L37`, disrupted epithelium, and the densest sensory innervation in the body |\n| **B. Excessive lacrimation** | Yes — reflex tearing from trigeminal stimulation; `L37` prints \"tearing\" in the mechanically-irritated cornea |\n| **C. Red eye** | Yes — the whole chapter is titled for it, and `L37`'s Table 4-2 gives keratitis a **ciliary flush** |\n| **D. Photophobia** | Yes — score 3 in the keratitis column |\n| **E. All of the above** | The key |\n\n⚠️ **The list is symptoms, not signs, and the missing one is worth noting.** Blurred vision scores 3 for keratitis in the same table and is the symptom the bank left out — a central foreign body reduces acuity. The management side is asked separately by `opmcq-c8-19`, and the deck's own line is \"Corneal foreign body: remove under slit lamp with 25G needle / burr; check for retained intraocular foreign body (CT scan)\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A corneal foreign body disrupts the epithelium of the body's most densely innervated tissue, so pain, reflex lacrimation, redness and photophobia all occur — the red-eye chapter scores photophobia 3 out of 3 for keratitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.56 (Ch.8 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following is used to detect corneal abrasions?",
    options:["Fluorescein dye","Steroids","Pachymetry","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The deck states it twice, once as anatomy and once as management.** `L15,16) Cornea`, the epithelium panel of \"The Five Layers of the Cornea\":\n\n> ① Epithelium (50 µm) · 5–6 cell layers thick · Regenerates completely in 5–7 days · Tight junctions → barrier function · **Stains with fluorescein when disrupted**\n\nand its trauma slide:\n\n> **Corneal abrasion: fluorescein staining delineates defect**; treat with topical antibiotic ± cycloplegic; usually heals in 24–48 h\n\n**Why it works is the barrier.** Fluorescein is water-soluble and the intact epithelium's tight junctions keep it out; wherever those cells are missing it pools in the exposed stroma and glows green under cobalt blue. So the dye does not stain the abrasion — it stains everywhere the abrasion is *not* covered.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Fluorescein dye** | Printed \"Flourescine dye\" in the bank. The key |\n| **B. Steroids** | A drug, not a test — and the wrong drug. `L15,16) Cornea` devotes a whole slide (\"Common Prescribing Errors in Corneal Disease\") to steroids potentiating infection, delaying healing and promoting viral replication. On a broken epithelium a steroid is a hazard, not an instrument |\n| **C. Pachymetry** | Measures corneal **thickness**. Printed in the deck's refractive-surgery must-checks — \"Pachymetry – minimum thickness\", \"central thickness <500 µm is a relative contraindication\". Thickness is a number about the whole cornea; it cannot localise a surface defect |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n⚠️ **The same dye answers a different question elsewhere in this bank, and the difference is what leaks.** `opmcq-c8-17` asks about the **Seidel** test, which is also fluorescein — but read for a stream of clear aqueous *diluting* the dye, which means a full-thickness perforation rather than an epithelial scrape. Same bottle, opposite depth.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain asks the fluorescein family four ways — `opqb-t8-299` (the stain is **green**), `opqb-t8-302` (a positive fluorescein test is the sure diagnostic sign of corneal ulcer), `opqb-t8-339` (fluorescein is **not** useful for a corneal **scar**, because a scar is epithelialised) and `opqb-t8-348` (fluorescein revealing diffuse punctate erosions in a welder). None of them is this stem.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Fluorescein detects corneal abrasions because the intact epithelium's tight junctions exclude the dye and any break lets it pool; pachymetry measures thickness and steroids are a hazard on a broken epithelium, not a test.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.56 (Ch.8 Q8; option A printed "Flourescine dye")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Herpes simplex virus can cause all of the following ocular conditions except?",
    options:["Blepharitis","Keratitis","Scleritis","Iridocyclitis","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The key is set by which herpesvirus the course attaches to the sclera, and the two are attached to different ones.**\n\n**Scleritis belongs to zoster in every source that names a herpesvirus at all.**\n\n- `ophthalmology.pdf` p.99, the causes of scleritis: \"Infections (TB, Syphilis, **Herpes zoster**, …etc)\" — zoster is named; simplex is not.\n- `L15,16) Cornea`, HZO complication box: epithelial keratitis · stromal / disciform keratitis · neurotrophic keratopathy · **scleritis / episcleritis** · post-herpetic neuralgia.\n- The same deck's HSV block — dendritic/geographic epithelial, necrotizing stromal, disciform — **never reaches the sclera**.\n\nThat asymmetry is the question.\n\n| Option | Where the course puts HSV |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Blepharitis** | `book/ch06-conjunctiva` names \"Herpes simplex virus follicular conjunctivitis (**blepharoconjunctivitis**)\", and `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 36 sets \"Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus \\| Herpes Simplex\" side by side, the simplex photograph showing a crop of vesicles on a red base **at the lash line**, some grouped among the lashes. Lid-margin herpes simplex is shown and named. True |\n| **B. Keratitis** | The deck's own section heading: \"Herpes Simplex Keratitis — **most common infectious cause of corneal blindness in developed countries**\". True, emphatically |\n| **C. Scleritis** | ⚠️ **FALSE. The key.** Not printed for HSV in any cached source |\n| **D. Iridocyclitis** | Anterior uveitis. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 11 lists the viral causes as \"**HSV**, HZV, CMV,….\", `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134 repeats it, and p.135 gives \"Trabeculitis as in **HSV** or HZV iritis\" as a cause of the raised IOP. True, and named three times |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails because C is false |\n\n**The trap is D, and the trap is that iritis feels like an over-reach.** A reader who has learned HSV purely as a corneal disease will pick iridocyclitis as the outlier and be wrong twice over — herpetic iritis is not only real, it is the reason the course prints a herpetic mechanism for secondary glaucoma.\n\n⚠️ **How the absence was measured, because \"HSV does not cause scleritis\" is a strong claim.** `scleritis` was grepped case-insensitively across all twenty-nine cached decks and all twelve cached book chapters. Every hit sits in one of three places: the sclera deck `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` and book chapter `ch07-sclera` (whose infective causes are TB, syphilis, herpes **zoster**), the HZO complication box in `L15,16) Cornea`, or a not-covered record. **No cached source pairs `simplex` with `scleritis` anywhere.** ⚠️ Outside this course, herpes simplex *is* a recognised cause of infectious scleritis — so the key is the course's position, not an absolute. **Not taken from the course material**, and recorded rather than used to dispute the key.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain asks the mirror question. `opqb-t8-321` lists the presentations of HSV keratitis and keys the exception as \"Central single ulcer with **satellite lesions**\" — the fungal sign — while `opqb-t8-304` and `opqb-t8-344` ask what characterises HSV keratitis (hypoesthesia; recurrence). None is this menu.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"This course gives herpes simplex the lid margin, the cornea and the iris, and gives the sclera to herpes zoster: scleritis is the odd one out because zoster, not simplex, appears in the printed list of infective scleritis causes.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.56 (Ch.8 Q9; ⚠️ the key "Answer: C" is orphaned onto the head of p.57)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"All of the following is among the layers of cornea except?",
    options:["Bowman's membrane","Descemet's membrane","Bruch's membrane","Endothelial cells","Stroma"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The deck prints the five layers in order and Bruch's is not one of them.** `L15,16) Cornea`, anatomy overview: \"**5 classic layers (epithelium → Bowman's → stroma → Descemet's → endothelium)**\", and the following slide expands each:\n\n| Layer | The deck's description |\n|---|---|\n| ① **Epithelium** (50 µm) | 5–6 cell layers · regenerates completely in 5–7 days · tight junctions → barrier · stains with fluorescein when disrupted |\n| ② **Bowman's layer** (12 µm) | Acellular, condensed collagen · **does NOT regenerate** if damaged |\n| ③ **Stroma** (90 % of thickness) | Orthogonally arranged collagen lamellae · keratocytes maintain matrix · damage → scarring and opacity |\n| ④ **Descemet's** | Basement membrane of the endothelium |\n| ⑤ **Endothelium** | Single cell layer, Na/K ATPase pump · **cannot regenerate**; cell density critical (2,500–3,000 cells/mm², oedema risk below 700) |\n\nThe question offers four of those five — Bowman's, Descemet's, endothelium, stroma — and one intruder.\n\n**Bruch's membrane belongs to the back of the eye.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` places it as the innermost layer of the **choroid**, between the choriocapillaris and the retinal pigment epithelium; `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` and `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` use it the same way, and `book/ch10-uveal-tract` prints it in the choroid's layer list. It is the surface that breaks in **angioid streaks** and the plane where drusen accumulate in age-related macular degeneration — the opposite pole of the globe from the cornea.\n\n**Why the distractor is well built.** Bowman's, Descemet's and Bruch's are all thin, acellular, collagen-and-elastin basement-membrane-like sheets named after nineteenth-century anatomists, and two of the three genuinely are corneal. The question is not testing the cornea so much as testing whether the third name has been filed in the right organ. *(The shared histological character of the three is **not taken from the course material** — the course names each in its own chapter and never compares them.)*\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t10-434` asks the same fact from the other end — \"**Bruch's membrane** is part of:\" — filed under `op-uvea`. Between the two, the pair is complete.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The cornea's five layers are epithelium, Bowman's, stroma, Descemet's and endothelium; Bruch's membrane is the innermost layer of the choroid and belongs to the posterior segment.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.57 (Ch.8 Q10)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following factors contributes to corneal transparency?",
    options:["Avascularity","Regular arrangement of collagen bundles","Relative dehydration of the cornea","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All three are printed, and they are printed in three different places on two consecutive slides.** `L15,16) Cornea`:\n\n> **Corneal Anatomy: Overview** — \"**Completely avascular**; nutrients supplied by aqueous humour (posterior) and tears (anterior)\"\n>\n> **The Five Layers** ③ Stroma — \"**Orthogonally arranged collagen lamellae**\"\n>\n> **Corneal Anatomy: Overview** — \"**Endothelium is critical for maintaining transparency via active fluid pumping**\", expanded in the layer panel as \"Endothelium: single cell layer, **Na/K ATPase pump**\" with \"Endothelial density: 2,500–3,000 cells/mm² · Critical density: <700 cells/mm² → **oedema risk**\"\n\n| Option | The mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Avascularity** | Blood vessels scatter and absorb light. The cornea is the only tissue of its size in the body with none, and it pays for that by taking nutrition from the aqueous behind and the tear film in front |\n| **B. Regular arrangement of collagen bundles** | Lamellae laid down orthogonally and uniformly spaced, so that light scattered by one fibril is cancelled by destructive interference from its neighbours. The deck's word is *orthogonally*; disorder is why \"damage → scarring & opacity\" |\n| **C. Relative dehydration** | **Deturgescence.** The endothelial Na/K ATPase pumps fluid out of the stroma against the constant leak inward. Lose the pump — Fuchs' dystrophy, endothelial cell counts below 700/mm² — and the stroma swells, the lamellar spacing goes irregular, and the cornea clouds. This is why the deck calls the endothelium \"critical for maintaining transparency\" |\n| **D. All of the above** | The key |\n\n**The three are not independent — C is the mechanism that keeps B true.** Regular spacing only stays regular while the stroma stays dry, which is why endothelial failure and stromal oedema are the same event described from two directions, and why \"worse vision in the morning\" is the Fuchs' symptom the deck prints twice: overnight lid closure stops evaporation, oedema peaks, and vision clears through the day.\n\n**Cross-bank, and this is the pair worth reading together.** Grade Gain asks the identical fact as an **EXCEPT** question twice, and the exceptions it chooses show what the course counts as *not* a transparency factor: `opqb-t8-311` keys **myelinated nerve fibres** (the corneal nerves are unmyelinated, precisely so they do not scatter light) and `opqb-t8-331` keys **a high population of goblet cells** (goblet cells are conjunctival; their mucin would be on the cornea, not in it). Between those two and this one, the same short list is tested from both sides.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Corneal transparency rests on avascularity, orthogonally arranged uniformly spaced stromal collagen, and deturgescence maintained by the endothelial Na/K ATPase pump — all three, and the third is what keeps the second true.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.57 (Ch.8 Q11)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most important and prevalent complication of herpes zoster ophthalmicus?",
    options:["Cicatrisation of skin","Postherpetic neuralgia","Limbal atrophy","Vision loss"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Post-herpetic neuralgia is the only HZO complication the deck prints twice.** `L15,16) Cornea`, HZO slide: it appears in the boxed complication list (epithelial keratitis · stromal/disciform keratitis · neurotrophic keratopathy · scleritis/episcleritis · **post-herpetic neuralgia**) and again in the slide's closing long-term line — \"Long-term: **post-herpetic neuralgia**, corneal anaesthesia (neurotrophic keratopathy)\". Nothing else on the slide is given twice, and the same slide sets the scale of the problem: \"**1 in 3** people will develop herpes zoster in their lifetime\".\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Cicatrisation of skin** | Real — it is the mechanism behind `opmcq-c8-5`'s entropion and `opmcq-c8-3`'s lid sequelae — but **not printed in any cached source as an HZO complication**, and skin scarring is disfiguring rather than the disease's defining morbidity |\n| **B. Postherpetic neuralgia** | The key. Printed twice on the deck's own HZO slide |\n| **C. Limbal atrophy** | The phrase appears in **no** cached deck or book chapter; it enters this chapter only as one limb of `opmcq-c8-3`'s \"scleral and limbal atrophy\". A late structural change, not a prevalent one |\n| **D. Vision loss** | ⚠️ **The option worth arguing with, and the argument is recorded rather than used to dispute the key** |\n\n⚠️ **WHY D IS DEFENSIBLE AND WHY IT IS STILL NOT THE KEY.** The deck's HZO corneal list is entirely sight-threatening — punctate epithelial keratitis, pseudodendritic ulcer, mucous plaques, disciform keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy — and neurotrophic keratopathy runs to \"persistent epithelial defect → **melting**\". A reader could argue vision loss is the *most important* outcome. Two things settle it for the printed key: the question pairs *important* with **prevalent**, and post-herpetic neuralgia is the complication that follows the largest share of cases whether or not the eye is involved; and the bank immediately asks its treatment in `opmcq-c8-13`, which is the pair that shows what it wanted here. **`answer` is the bank's printed key; the disagreement is noted, not corrected.**\n\n**What post-herpetic neuralgia actually is.** Persistent burning, lancinating or allodynic pain in the affected dermatome, lasting beyond the healing of the rash — often months, sometimes years — caused by damage to the sensory ganglion and its central connections rather than by continuing viral replication. That is why the treatment in `opmcq-c8-13` is a neuropathic-pain agent and not an antiviral or an analgesic. *(The pathophysiology and the time course are **not taken from the course material**: the deck names the entity in two lines and defines it nowhere.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Post-herpetic neuralgia is the complication the deck prints twice on its own HZO slide and the one that follows the largest share of cases; sight-threatening corneal disease is more serious in the eye it affects but less prevalent overall.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.57 (Ch.8 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following is the best treatment of postherpetic neuralgia?",
    options:["NSAIDs","Acetaminophen","Massive doses of steroids","Gabapentin"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THE COURSE DOES NOT ANSWER THIS QUESTION, AND THE GAP IS FILLED RATHER THAN DECLARED.** How it was measured: `gabapentin`, `pregabalin`, `amitriptyline`, `acetaminophen`, `paracetamol` and `capsaicin` each return **exactly zero hits across all twenty-nine cached lecture files and all twelve cached book chapters**, case-insensitively. `L15,16) Cornea` names post-herpetic neuralgia twice on its HZO slide and prescribes for it **nothing at all** — the only drug that slide carries is \"oral valaciclovir/famciclovir within 72 h of rash onset\", which is treatment of the acute attack and prophylaxis against the neuralgia, not treatment of it once established.\n\n**Why gabapentin, from general medical knowledge (not taken from the course material).** Post-herpetic neuralgia is **neuropathic** pain — the sensory ganglion and its central connections are damaged, and the pain generator is abnormal ectopic firing in injured neurons rather than tissue inflammation. Gabapentin binds the α2δ subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels on those neurons and damps the firing. Gabapentin, pregabalin and the tricyclics are the first-line agents in every standard guideline; capsaicin and lidocaine patches are topical alternatives.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **A. NSAIDs** | Anti-inflammatory analgesia. There is no inflammation left to treat once the rash has healed — nociceptive drugs are poorly effective in neuropathic pain, which is the single most useful thing to know about this diagnosis |\n| **B. Acetaminophen** | Same objection, one step weaker. A simple analgesic acting centrally on nociceptive pain |\n| **C. Massive doses of steroids** | ⚠️ Note the phrasing — this bank uses \"massive doses of steroids\" as a distractor **four times in three chapters** (`opmcq-c7-2`, `opmcq-c8-20`, and here), and it has never once been the key. Steroids do not treat established neuropathic pain, and `L15,16) Cornea` devotes a whole slide to the harms of steroids in corneal disease |\n| **D. Gabapentin** | The key |\n\n**The distinction the question is really testing.** Nociceptive pain responds to NSAIDs and paracetamol; neuropathic pain responds to anticonvulsants and tricyclics. Three of these four options are the wrong *category* of analgesic, and only one recognises what kind of pain is being treated.\n\n⚠️ **Read with `opmcq-c8-4`.** The best treatment of post-herpetic neuralgia in practice is to have started the antiviral inside 72 hours, which is that question's key — the deck's own claim that oral aciclovir prophylaxis \"reduces recurrence rate by ~50 %\" belongs to recurrent HSV rather than to zoster, so the prevention claim here is **not taken from the course material** either.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Post-herpetic neuralgia is neuropathic pain and is treated with a neuropathic-pain agent — gabapentin (or pregabalin, or a tricyclic); NSAIDs, paracetamol and steroids are the wrong category of analgesic.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.57 (Ch.8 Q13)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-214', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following is described as dust-like calcium deposits in the corneal subepithelium, Bowman's layer and anterior stroma?",
    options:["Arcus senilis","Keratoconus","Band keratopathy","Exposure keratopathy","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION IS PRINTED WITH THE NUMBER \"214.\"** — verified at 400 dpi on book p.57. It sits between the printed 13 and the printed 14, and **the very next question is also numbered 14**, so the chapter has two questions in position fourteen. The id keeps the printed number rather than renaming a question the book numbers 14. ⚠️ Its key \"Answer: C\" is orphaned onto the head of p.58. The stem's parentheses and lower-case \"bowman's\" are the bank's; only the capitalisation is repaired here.\n\n**The deck defines it in one line and gives it a cause list in a box.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Corneal Degenerations\":\n\n> **Band keratopathy: calcium deposits in Bowman's layer in the interpalpebral zone**; associated with chronic ocular disease, hypercalcaemia, chronic uveitis\n>\n> **Band Keratopathy Causes** — Chronic anterior uveitis · Hypercalcaemia · Silicone oil in AC · End-stage ocular disease · Gout (hyperuricaemia) · Familial / hereditary · Idiopathic age-related\n>\n> Treatment: **EDTA chelation** (effective) or excimer laser **PTK**\n\nThe stem's \"calcium\", \"Bowman's layer\" and the deposits' dust-like character all sit in that line. ⚠️ The stem's **\"subepithelium … and anterior stroma\"** extends the plane beyond what the deck prints — the deck confines the calcium to Bowman's. The wider distribution is standard and is **not taken from the course material**; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 corroborates only the association, listing \"**Calcific band keratopathy** (if the iritis becomes chronic) especially in juvenile idiopathic arthritis\" among the complications of anterior uveitis.\n\n| Option | What it actually is | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Arcus senilis** | \"Age-related: Arcus senilis (**lipid** arc at limbus – physiological in elderly, check lipids if <45 yrs)\", same deck slide | **Lipid**, not calcium; **limbal**, not interpalpebral; an arc, not a band. The chapter asks it directly at `opmcq-c8-20` and `opmcq-c8-21` |\n| **B. Keratoconus** | Progressive corneal thinning and protrusion | A shape change with no deposit of any kind |\n| **C. Band keratopathy** | The key |\n| **D. Exposure keratopathy** | \"Incomplete lid closure → inferior epithelial breakdown\" from VII palsy, thyroid eye disease, proptosis | ⚠️ **The subtlest distractor, because it shares the geography.** Both diseases live in the exposed strip of cornea — band keratopathy in the interpalpebral zone because that is where tear evaporation concentrates calcium, exposure keratopathy in the **inferior** cornea because that is what an unclosed lid leaves uncovered. Neither deposits calcium |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails |\n\n**Why the band sits where it does.** The interpalpebral strip is the part of the cornea the lids never cover, so its tear film evaporates fastest; the local pH rises and calcium phosphate precipitates in the most superficial acellular layer available, which is Bowman's. That is also why the band starts at the 3 and 9 o'clock periphery with a clear interval at the limbus and grows inward. *(The evaporation-and-pH mechanism is **not taken from the course material** — the deck states the location and the causes without a mechanism.)*\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain splits this into its two component facts — `opqb-t8-297` keys the deposit (**calcium salt**) and `opqb-t8-322` keys the site (**interpalpebral part of the cornea**). This question asks for the name given both, and `opmcq-c8-28` asks its treatment.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Dust-like calcium in the subepithelium and Bowman's layer of the interpalpebral cornea is band keratopathy — arcus senilis is lipid at the limbus and exposure keratopathy is an inferior epithelial breakdown with no deposit.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.57 (Ch.8, the question printed "214." between Q13 and Q14; ⚠️ its key "Answer: C" is orphaned onto the head of p.58)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:"A 21-year-old male patient complains of diminished vision in the left eye. Examination revealed positive Munson's sign in the affected eye. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Herpes zoster ophthalmicus","Calcific band keratopathy","Photokeratitis","Keratoconus","Corneal ulcer"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Munson's sign is defined once in this course, and only for one disease.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Keratoconus: Diagnosis & Management\":\n\n> **Munson's sign: V-shaped deformity of lower lid on downgaze**\n\nIt is a mechanical consequence and nothing else: the cone pushes the lower lid forward as the eye rotates down, so the lid margin, which normally follows a smooth arc, kinks into a V. The sign is therefore a sign of **corneal shape**, and no other condition on this menu changes corneal shape.\n\n**The age fits the printed natural history.** The same slide: \"Onset: **puberty**; progression until 3rd–4th decade\" — a 21-year-old is inside the progressive window, which is also why `opmcq-c8-15` asks how to measure the progression.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Herpes zoster ophthalmicus** | Painful, unilateral, dermatomal, with a rash. The stem gives painless diminished vision and one lid sign |\n| **B. Calcific band keratopathy** | Calcium in Bowman's layer in the interpalpebral zone — an opacity, not a protrusion. It cannot deform the lid |\n| **C. Photokeratitis** | ⚠️ **The word \"photokeratitis\" does not appear in this course.** Measured: `photokerat` returns **zero hits across all twenty-nine cached decks and all twelve cached book chapters**. The entity is taught under a different name — `L26) Trauma II` files it under \"Physical Injuries · **UV Rays** · **Photophthalmia** · Snow blindness\", and `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` slide 74 notes occupation matters because \"**Welders: FB & photophobia**\". Whatever it is called, it is an acute, painful, bilateral surface burn hours after unprotected UV exposure — not a chronic unilateral shape change *(the clinical description is **not taken from the course material**; the course prints the name and the cause without a clinical picture)* |\n| **D. Keratoconus** | The key |\n| **E. Corneal ulcer** | Painful, red, fluorescein-positive. `L37) Red Eye` scores keratitis pain 2 and photophobia 3 out of 3. The stem describes none of it |\n\n**How the sign is elicited, since the bank asks about it twice.** Ask the patient to look down while you watch the lower lid from the side or from below: the cone indents the lid into an angle. ⚠️ **The chapter asks the same sign from the opposite direction at `opmcq-c8-24`** — an 11-year-old with Down's syndrome and \"angulation of the lower eyelid upon looking downwards\", asking for the sign's *name*. Read the two together: this one gives the name and asks the disease; that one gives the finding and asks the name.\n\n**Filed in `op-cornea-surg`** — *\"Cornea — ectasia, grafts and refractive surgery\"* — with `opmcq-c8-6`, `opmcq-c8-15`, `opmcq-c8-24` and `opmcq-c8-27`.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t8-313` keys the **Kayser-Fleischer ring** as the non-keratoconus item in a list that includes Munson's sign, and `opqb-t8-314` keys \"All of the above\" over Munson's sign, diagnostic topography and corneal opacity. Neither asks the diagnosis from the sign alone.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Munson's sign — a V-shaped deformity of the lower lid on downgaze — is a sign of corneal protrusion and belongs to keratoconus, which presents with painless progressive blurring from puberty into the fourth decade.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.58 (Ch.8 Q14; stem printed "diminshed"; ⚠️ this is the SECOND question in the chapter to be numbered 14 — see the question printed "214." on p.57)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:"A 21-year-old man has diminished vision in the left eye and a positive Munson's sign in that eye. Which of the following may be used to accurately assess the patient's condition?",
    options:["Corneal topography","Pachymetry","Pentacam","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the prevoius case, which of the following may be used to accurately assess the patient's condition?\"** (\"prevoius\" is the bank's). The antecedent is `opmcq-c8-14`, printed immediately above on book p.58, and it was read rather than assumed. The restatement carries that patient's **findings** only — the word *keratoconus* is withheld, because naming it would answer `opmcq-c8-14`.\n\n**Two of the three are printed by name and the third is the machine that performs both.** `L15,16) Cornea`:\n\n> **Topography**: inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie pattern *(keratoconus diagnosis slide)*\n>\n> Screening for ectasia is **MANDATORY** before refractive surgery (**corneal topography**) *(ectasia slide)*\n>\n> Pre-op Must-Checks — **Corneal topography** – RULE OUT ectasia · **Pachymetry** – minimum thickness *(refractive surgery slide)*\n>\n> **Pachymetry**: central thickness <500 µm is a relative contraindication; RSB <300 µm = absolute contraindication for LASIK *(screening slide)*\n\n| Option | What it measures | Why it belongs here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Corneal topography** | Anterior surface **curvature**, mapped in dioptres | The diagnosis itself: inferior steepening and the asymmetric bowtie are the printed pattern. Also how progression is documented, which decides whether cross-linking is offered |\n| **B. Pachymetry** | Corneal **thickness**, in microns | The other half of ectasia — keratoconus is defined by the deck as \"thinning **and** protrusion\", and the thinnest-point value and its location track the cone |\n| **C. Pentacam** | Both, plus the **posterior** surface | ⚠️ Not named anywhere in this course — `pentacam` returns **zero hits across twenty-nine cached decks and twelve cached book chapters**. A rotating-Scheimpflug tomographer photographing the cornea through 360°, reconstructing anterior and posterior elevation and a full pachymetry map from one scan. **Not taken from the course material** |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | The key |\n| **E. None of the above** | — | Fails |\n\n**Why \"all of the above\" is right rather than lazy here.** These are not three competing tests; they are curvature, thickness, and the instrument that measures both together. The deck's own pre-op checklist demands topography *and* pachymetry as separate line items, so a reader who picks one has answered half the printed requirement.\n\n⚠️ **What the deck says the assessment is FOR.** \"CXL does **NOT** improve vision – it **stabilizes** progression\", and the indication is \"documented **progressive** keratoconus in young patients\". The word *documented* is why serial imaging is the management tool and not merely the diagnostic one — you cannot show progression without a baseline. The management ladder that follows is spectacles → soft/rigid contact lenses → scleral lenses → CXL → intracorneal ring segments → keratoplasty (DALK preferred over PKP), and \"**Avoid eye rubbing!**\".\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t8-341` keys **computerized topography** as the *earliest* indicator of keratoconus — the same tool, asked as a single best rather than as a set.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Keratoconus is assessed by curvature and thickness together — topography for the inferior steepening and asymmetric bowtie, pachymetry for the thinning, and Scheimpflug tomography for both plus the posterior surface; serial imaging is what documents the progression that indicates cross-linking.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.58 (Ch.8 Q15; printed "In the prevoius case, which of the following may be used to accurately assess the patient\'s condition?" and refers back to `opmcq-c8-14`. ⚠️ THIS CHAPTER PRINTS TWO QUESTIONS NUMBERED 14 — the one printed "214." between Q13 and Q14, held as `opmcq-c8-214`, is NOT the antecedent. The antecedent was read, not assumed: it is the keratoconus case printed "14.")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding Hutchinson's sign except?",
    options:["It's applied in case of herpes zoster ophthalmicus","It states that eye is involved in 75% of the cases if tip of nose is involved","It's applied in case of nasociliary nerve affection","It's a highly sensitive and specific sign"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The sign is defined in exactly one line in this whole course, and that line settles A and C.** `L15,16) Cornea`, HZO slide:\n\n> **Hutchinson's sign: vesicles on tip/side of nose → nasociliary branch involved → HIGH RISK of ocular complications**\n\nThree clauses: the disease (zoster), the nerve (nasociliary), and the inference (raised risk). Options A and C are that line split in two.\n\n**The anatomy behind it.** The **nasociliary** nerve is a branch of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal (V1). It supplies the tip of the nose through its external nasal branch — and it also gives off the **long ciliary nerves** to the globe. One nerve, two territories. So vesicles at the nose tip mean the virus has reactivated in a ganglion segment whose fibres also reach the eye. *(The branching detail is **not taken from the course material**: `trigeminal` returns three cached files and all three treat it only as the cornea's sensory supply.)*\n\n| Option | True? | Where it comes from |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Applied in herpes zoster ophthalmicus** | True | Printed. The sign exists only in HZO |\n| **B. Eye involved in 75 % of cases if the nose tip is involved** | ⚠️ **Not printed anywhere in this course** — the deck says \"HIGH risk\" and gives no number. Measured: no cached deck or book chapter attaches a percentage to Hutchinson's sign. The classic figures quoted in the literature run roughly 50–76 %, so 75 % is within the accepted range. **Not taken from the course material**, and the bank treats it as true | Outside knowledge |\n| **C. Applied in nasociliary nerve affection** | True | Printed, verbatim |\n| **D. Highly sensitive and specific** | ⚠️ **FALSE. The key** | See below |\n\n**Why D is the exception.** A sign is *specific* if its presence reliably means disease and *sensitive* if its absence reliably excludes it. Hutchinson's sign is neither in the strong sense the option claims. It is a **risk marker**, which is exactly the word the deck chose: a positive sign raises the probability of ocular involvement, but a large minority of patients with nose-tip vesicles never develop eye disease, and — the clinically dangerous half — **a substantial share of patients with ocular zoster have no nose-tip lesion at all**. That is why every ophthalmologist examines the eye in HZO regardless of the nose. *(The sensitivity and specificity judgement is **not taken from the course material**; the course prints only \"HIGH risk\".)*\n\n**How to hold the whole question.** The deck says *high risk*, the bank says *not highly sensitive and specific*, and those are the same statement said twice: a risk factor is not a diagnostic test.\n\n⚠️ **A source note.** Hutchinson's sign is taught in **`L15,16) Cornea` and nowhere else in this module.** How that was measured: `hutchinson` returns three cached files — this deck, `L5) Eyelid disease`, and `book/ch05-eyelids` — and the second and third hits are both inside those files' own NOT-COVERED records (\"'Hutchinson' returns zero in the typed text\"; \"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus, Hutchinson's sign, eyelid herpes simplex — ABSENT\"). `L5` slide 36 **photographs** the nose-tip eruption without naming the sign. One real teaching source, two records of absence.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t8-343` asks the sign without its eponym — \"the appearance of the following means a high possibility for corneal affection\", keying **eruption on the tip of the nose**. Same fact, no name, no percentage.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hutchinson's sign — vesicles on the tip or side of the nose in herpes zoster ophthalmicus — marks nasociliary involvement and a high risk of ocular disease, but it is a risk marker rather than a sensitive and specific test, so a clear nose never excludes eye involvement.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.58 (Ch.8 Q16)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following is the use of Seidel test?",
    options:["To detect corneal scarring","To detect corneal perforation","To detect corneal foreign body","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The test is taught in the trauma deck, twice, and both times as a test for a leak.** `L25) Trauma I`:\n\n> Slide 50 — Signs of suspected open globe may include: peaked pupil · shallow or deep anterior chamber · bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage · **a positive SEIDEL TEST will help detect leaking aqueous**\n>\n> Slide 61 — **SEIDEL TESTING** · Assess for **corneal break** · Apply fluorescein to the suspected area · Normally appears \"green\" under cobalt blue light · **Dye turns \"clear\" when a break is present as aqueous dilutes fluorescein**\n\n**The mechanism is the whole of the answer.** Concentrated fluorescein under cobalt blue is dark orange-green; dilute it and it fluoresces bright green, then clear. A full-thickness corneal wound leaks aqueous, and the leaking stream washes a **clear rivulet** down through the dye — visible movement, not a static stain. That is why the test detects a **perforation** specifically: a partial-thickness defect stains but does not run.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Corneal scarring** | A scar is fully epithelialised and does not take up dye at all — Grade Gain keys exactly this at `opqb-t8-339` (\"Fluorescein test is **not useful** in… the diagnosis of corneal scar\") |\n| **B. Corneal perforation** | The key. Printed \"conreal perforation\" in the bank |\n| **C. Corneal foreign body** | A foreign body is found by **looking**, at the slit lamp — the deck's line is \"remove under slit lamp with 25G needle / burr\", and it lists lid eversion for subtarsal bodies. Fluorescein may show the surrounding abrasion, but nothing about the Seidel manoeuvre is aimed at locating a speck |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails on A and C |\n| **E. None of the above** | Fails because B is printed |\n\n⚠️ **Where a positive Seidel takes you is the reason it matters.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 51: \"If you suspect an open globe or the globe is ruptured — place **EYE SHIELD** and CALL for OPHTHALMOLOGIST immediately · **DO NOT PLACE A PATCH**, as pressure can extrude intraocular contents · **DO NOT CHECK EYE PRESSURE**.\" A positive Seidel test converts a red eye into a surgical emergency, and two of the reflex actions a clinician would otherwise take become contraindicated.\n\n⚠️ **The same dye, three different readings, and this bank tests two of them.** `opmcq-c8-8` reads fluorescein statically for an epithelial break (abrasion); this question reads it dynamically for a leak (perforation). `book/ch01-examination` lists the Seidel test among the techniques its own examination sequence does **not** teach (item 25 of its not-covered list) — so the trauma deck is the module's only source for it, and `seidel` returns exactly two cached files.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The Seidel test applies fluorescein and watches for leaking aqueous to dilute it into a clear stream, which detects a full-thickness corneal perforation; a scar takes no dye and a foreign body is found by looking.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.58 (Ch.8 Q17; option B printed "To detect conreal perforation")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Deficiency of which of the following vitamins can cause corneal ulcers?",
    options:["Vitamin A","Vitamin B","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THE OPTION LETTERS ARE MISPRINTED AND THE LIST STRADDLES A PAGE BREAK.** `ophthalmology MCQ.pdf` prints four options lettered **A, B, C, E** — the letter D is skipped and \"Vitamin D\" is lettered E. The list is also split across the seam: \"A. Vitamin A\" and \"B. Vitamin B\" are the last two lines of book p.58, and \"C. Vitamin C\", \"E. Vitamin D\" and \"Answer: A\" open p.59. Both ends were read. The printed key A is unaffected by the letter skip, and the entry ships the four options in printed order.\n\n**Vitamin A deficiency is a printed risk factor for bacterial keratitis in this course.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Bacterial Keratitis: Risk Factors & Common Organisms\" lists, under risk factors:\n\n> Systemic immunosuppression, diabetes, **vitamin A deficiency**\n\n⚠️ **That line is easy to miss with a grep and it was read, not grepped.** In the cached text the phrase is broken across a line — \"diabetes, vitamin\" / \"A deficiency\" — so a line-based search for \"vitamin A\" returns only `L6) Conjunctiva` (inside that deck's own not-covered record, which states \"'vitamin A' = 0\") and `book/ch04-lacrimal-system`. The cornea deck's hit is real and would have been written off as an absence by an unopened grep.\n\n**Two mechanisms, and the course prints both halves separately.**\n\n| Mechanism | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **Surface drying.** `book/ch04-lacrimal-system` lists \"**Vitamin A deficiency**\" among the causes of **aqueous-deficient dry eye**, alongside Sjögren's, diabetes and post-refractive-surgery. A dry surface breaks down | Printed |\n| **Loss of goblet cells and keratinisation of the epithelium**, so the ocular surface loses its mucin and its barrier and progresses conjunctival xerosis → corneal xerosis → **keratomalacia**, a frank corneal melt | **Not taken from the course material** |\n\n⚠️ **The classic sign is photographed in this course and never named.** `L6) Conjunctiva` slide 62 is titled **Bitôt Spot** — the foamy keratinised patch of vitamin A deficiency — and that deck's own audit records that the slide \"does NOT mention vitamin A, measles or malnutrition (measles = 0, malnutrition = 0, xero* = 0, keratomalacia = 0)\". The sign is shown; the deficiency behind it is left unstated.\n\n| Option | Ocular deficiency disease | Corneal ulcer? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Vitamin A** | Night blindness, xerophthalmia, Bitôt spots, keratomalacia | **Yes — the key** |\n| **B. Vitamin B** | Thiamine deficiency gives Wernicke's ophthalmoplegia and nystagmus; B12 gives a nutritional optic neuropathy | No corneal ulceration. **Not taken from the course material** |\n| **C. Vitamin C** | Scurvy — subconjunctival and orbital haemorrhage from capillary fragility, and impaired collagen synthesis so wounds heal poorly | Delays healing rather than causing the ulcer. **Not taken from the course material** |\n| **D. Vitamin D** | Rickets; ocular associations are minimal | No |\n\n**The discriminating idea.** Vitamin A is the vitamin the **epithelium** needs to stay an epithelium. The others act on nerve, on collagen and on bone.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Vitamin A deficiency is a printed risk factor for bacterial keratitis and a cause of aqueous-deficient dry eye; it keratinises the ocular surface and destroys goblet cells, running xerophthalmia through to keratomalacia.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.58-59 (Ch.8 Q18; ⚠️ four options printed with the letters A, B, C, E — the letter D is skipped — and the list is split across the page break, options A and B on p.58, options C and E plus the key on p.59)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"All of the following are among the complications of corneal foreign body except?",
    options:["Rust ring","Corneal scarring","Infectious keratitis","Cataract"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three of the four are printed on the deck's corneal-trauma slide or one line above it.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Corneal Trauma: Approach & Management\":\n\n> Corneal foreign body: remove under slit lamp with 25G needle / burr; check for retained intraocular foreign body (CT scan)\n>\n> **Rust ring: after iron FB; remove at 24 h with burr (softens overnight)**\n\nand from the layers slide, \"③ Stroma — damage → **scarring & opacity**\", with the risk-factor slide listing \"**Ocular trauma** (agricultural injury – developing world)\" first among the causes of bacterial keratitis.\n\n| Option | Mechanism | Printed? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Rust ring** | A ferrous foreign body oxidises in the tear film and stains the surrounding stroma orange-brown within hours. The deck gives it its own line and its own timing — leave it 24 h and it softens, then take it with a burr | Yes, verbatim |\n| **B. Corneal scarring** | A foreign body that breaches **Bowman's layer** — \"acellular, condensed collagen · **does NOT regenerate** if damaged\" — or reaches the stroma leaves permanent opacity. An epithelium-only abrasion heals clear in 24–48 h because the epithelium \"regenerates completely in 5–7 days\". The depth decides the scar | Yes |\n| **C. Infectious keratitis** | The foreign body carries organisms in and destroys the barrier that would keep them out. Ocular trauma is the deck's first-listed risk factor for bacterial keratitis, and \"agricultural injury\" is the one it flags for **fungal** disease — \"Filamentous fungi (Aspergillus, Fusarium): **trauma with plant matter**\" | Yes |\n| **D. Cataract** | ⚠️ **The key.** A **corneal** foreign body sits in or on the cornea and never reaches the lens. Nothing in the deck's corneal-trauma block mentions the lens | No |\n\n⚠️ **WHY D IS ALMOST TRUE, AND WHY THE ADJECTIVE IS THE QUESTION.** The very next clause of the same printed line is \"**check for retained intraocular foreign body (CT scan)**\", and an *intraocular* foreign body that passes through the cornea into the lens absolutely does cause a traumatic cataract — `L26) Trauma II` teaches traumatic and radiation cataract, and `L18) Cataract II` teaches traumatic cataract as a type. So the option is false only because the stem says **corneal**. A reader who reasons from \"metal in the eye\" rather than from \"metal in the cornea\" will take it. This is the same discipline the whole deck line teaches: having removed the corneal body, you still have to rule out a second one inside.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain's foreign-body questions come at it from management rather than complications — `opqb-t8-348` keys **topical anaesthetic drops** as the thing never to give a welder with punctate erosions, and `opqb-t8-310` keys **fungal** corneal ulcer for an injury with a leaf. Neither is this list.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A corneal foreign body can leave a rust ring, a permanent scar if it breaches Bowman's layer, and infectious keratitis; cataract requires an intraocular foreign body, which is why the deck's next instruction is to look for one.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.59 (Ch.8 Q19)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 65-year-old man visits the ophthalmologist with a complaint of noticing a brown ring in both eyes. From history, there was no blurred vision or any other complaint. Examination revealed bilateral grayish brown ring around the limbus and separated from the limbus by a clear scleral zone. Which of the following would be the most important next step to do for such a patient?",
    options:["Urgent surgery","CT orbit","Lipid profile and reassurance","Massive steroid administration","Ophthalmoscopic examination"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**One printed line answers the whole question.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Corneal Degenerations\":\n\n> Age-related: **Arcus senilis (lipid arc at limbus – physiological in elderly, check lipids if <45 yrs)**\n\nThat single parenthesis contains both halves of the key — **check lipids**, and **it is physiological**, which is the reassurance.\n\n**Every clinical detail in the stem is the entity's definition.** Bilateral, at the limbus, separated from the limbus by a clear interval, and — decisively — **asymptomatic**: the arc is peripheral, so it never crosses the visual axis and never blurs vision. That is the finding that removes urgency from the case.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Urgent surgery** | Nothing is threatened. The deposit is peripheral, stable and outside the visual axis |\n| **B. CT orbit** | The orbit is not involved. The cornea deck's only CT indication is a retained intraocular foreign body |\n| **C. Lipid profile and reassurance** | The key, and both nouns matter |\n| **D. Massive steroid administration** | ⚠️ The bank's recurring null option — it appears as a distractor across three chapters (`opmcq-c7-2`, `opmcq-c8-13`, here) and has never been a key. There is no inflammation: arcus is a lipid deposit, not an infiltrate |\n| **E. Ophthalmoscopic examination** | Examines the fundus. Reasonable at any check-up, but it looks in the wrong place for this finding and answers no question the stem raises |\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THE KEY AND THE DECK DIVERGE ON WHETHER THIS PATIENT NEEDS THE LIPIDS CHECKED, AND THE DIVERGENCE IS RECORDED, NOT CORRECTED.** The deck's threshold is explicit — **\"check lipids if <45 yrs\"** — and this patient is **65**. On the deck's own rule, arcus at 65 is physiological and a lipid profile is not indicated by the eye finding at all; the whole of the answer would be the reassurance. The bank nevertheless keys \"Lipid profile **and** reassurance\", and it is still by a distance the best option on the menu: the other four are an unnecessary operation, the wrong imaging, a harmful drug and an examination of the wrong structure. **`answer` is the bank's printed key; the age-threshold disagreement is noted.** ⚠️ The reason the threshold exists is worth carrying: arcus **before** middle age (arcus **juvenilis**, which this chapter offers as a distractor at `opmcq-c8-21`) can signal familial hyperlipidaemia, and *that* is when the eye finding is a systemic lead.\n\n⚠️ **One more divergence, smaller.** The stem calls the clear interval a \"clear **scleral** zone\". The clear interval of arcus lies between the arc and the limbus **within the cornea** — it is a strip of unaffected peripheral cornea, not sclera. Recorded; the key does not turn on it.\n\n**This is the antecedent of `opmcq-c8-21`**, which asks the diagnosis. Read them together.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t8-346` gives the same patient thirteen years older — a 68-year-old with a bilateral white-grey peripheral ring and a clear zone, no visual complaints — and asks what is true of it, keying \"**Usually asymptomatic**\". Grade Gain asks what the finding *is like*; House asks what to *do about it*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A bilateral asymptomatic greyish ring at the limbus with a clear interval is arcus senilis — physiological in the elderly, needing reassurance, with a lipid profile indicated when the patient is under 45.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.59 (Ch.8 Q20)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 65-year-old man noticed a brown ring in both eyes. He has no blurred vision and no other complaint. Examination reveals a bilateral greyish-brown ring around the limbus, separated from the limbus by a clear zone. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Keratoconus","Corneal ulcer","Arcus senilis","Arcus juvenilis"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, what is the most likely diagnosis?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c8-20`, printed immediately above it on book p.59, and it was read rather than assumed. The restatement above carries that patient's **findings only**. Two things are deliberately withheld: the lipid profile and the reassurance, which are `opmcq-c8-20`'s own key, and the word *physiological*, which would hand over this question's answer inside its own stem.\n\n**Four printed characteristics, and the deck's line matches all four.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Corneal Degenerations\":\n\n> Age-related: **Arcus senilis (lipid arc at limbus – physiological in elderly, check lipids if <45 yrs)**\n\n| Finding | Fits arcus |\n|---|---|\n| Bilateral | A metabolic deposit, not a focal lesion |\n| **At the limbus** | \"Lipid arc at **limbus**\" — the deck's own word |\n| Separated by a clear interval | The peripheral-most cornea is spared, so the arc never actually touches the limbal edge |\n| **Asymptomatic** | Peripheral, outside the visual axis, so no acuity loss and no pain |\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Keratoconus** | Central protrusion and thinning, symptomatic from puberty, with Munson's sign and Vogt's striae. No ring, and the vision is never normal — the chapter's keratoconus questions are `opmcq-c8-6`, `opmcq-c8-14`, `opmcq-c8-15`, `opmcq-c8-24` and `opmcq-c8-27` |\n| **B. Corneal ulcer** | Acutely painful, red, unilateral, fluorescein-positive. `L37) Red Eye` scores keratitis photophobia 3 out of 3 and blurred vision 3 out of 3; this patient has neither |\n| **C. Arcus senilis** | The key |\n| **D. Arcus juvenilis** | ⚠️ **The one real competitor, and it fails on a single word in the stem: the age.** Printed \"Arcus juveniles\". The lesion is morphologically identical — the *same* lipid arc — and the two are distinguished by **when** they appear, not by how they look. Arcus in a young patient is arcus juvenilis and is the finding that raises familial hyperlipidaemia, which is exactly why the deck sets a threshold at 45 years. In a 65-year-old the arc is senile by definition |\n\n**What arcus actually is.** Lipid — cholesterol, cholesteryl esters and phospholipids — deposited in the peripheral corneal stroma, arriving through the limbal vessels and diffusing inward until the avascular cornea stops it. That is why it starts as separate superior and inferior arcs, why they later join into a full ring, and why a strip of clear cornea remains between the deposit and the vascular limbus. *(The composition and the diffusion account are **not taken from the course material**: `L15,16) Cornea` prints \"lipid arc at limbus\" and stops. Measured — `arcus` returns four cached files once \"Marcus\" is excluded, and the other three are `arcus marginalis`, an orbital septum structure, and a limbal ring in a normal-iris description. **This deck is the module's only source for arcus senilis.**)*\n\n⚠️ **Note the trap in the stem's own colour word.** The patient reports a **brown** ring and the examiner records **greyish-brown** — a description that could also fit a Kayser-Fleischer ring (copper, in Wilson's disease), which Grade Gain uses as a keratoconus distractor at `opqb-t8-313`. The K-F ring is not on this menu, but the discriminator is the same one: K-F copper sits in **Descemet's** membrane and has **no** clear interval at the limbus.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A bilateral asymptomatic greyish-brown limbal ring with a clear interval in an elderly patient is arcus senilis; the identical arc under about 45 is arcus juvenilis and is the one that flags familial hyperlipidaemia.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.59 (Ch.8 Q21; printed "In the previous case, what is the most likely diagnosis?" and refers back to Ch.8 Q20; option D printed "Arcus juveniles")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 29-year-old woman complains of eye redness, pain and blurred vision. After staining with fluorescein, examination revealed a dendritic ulcer in the affected eye. Which of the following is absolutely contraindicated in the treatment of that patient?",
    options:["Topical antiviral medications","Topical steroids","Prophylactic antibiotics","All of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The deck states this as a rule, in capitals, on a slide of its own.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Herpes Simplex Keratitis: Critical Point\":\n\n> **CLINICAL PEARL — NEVER use topical steroids in EPITHELIAL herpes keratitis – this promotes viral replication and can result in corneal perforation.**\n\nand it says it a second time on the prescribing-errors slide (\"Topical Steroids in Epithelial HSV — Promotes viral replication · **Can cause geographic ulcer** · Risk of perforation\") and a third time in the dendritic-ulcer scenario (\"Do NOT use steroids at this stage → risk of **geographic ulcer** → perforation\"). Three slides, one instruction.\n\n**A dendritic ulcer means active virus in the epithelium.** The deck's epithelial-HSV panel: \"**Active virus replication in the epithelium** · Classic dendritic ulcer with terminal buds · Fluorescein: stains the ulcer bed\". A steroid suppresses the local immune response that is containing that replication, so the lesion enlarges — which is precisely what `opmcq-c8-26` asks about two questions later.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Topical antiviral medications** | The **treatment**, not a contraindication: \"Topical antiviral (aciclovir 3 % ointment 5×/day or ganciclovir gel)\". `opmcq-c8-23` keys it |\n| **B. Topical steroids** | The key |\n| **C. Prophylactic antibiotics** | Neither indicated nor forbidden. Any epithelial defect can be secondarily infected, so cover is common practice; the deck prescribes topical antibiotic for a corneal abrasion and says nothing against it here |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails, because A is the treatment |\n\n⚠️ **THE RULE HAS A BOUNDARY AND THE BOUNDARY IS THE WHOLE POINT.** The deck permits steroids in the *other* form of the same disease: \"**Disciform (Stromal Immune)** — immune-mediated endotheliitis · central disc-like oedema · KPs under the area of oedema · **Treat with TOPICAL STEROIDS + antivirals**\", and \"Steroids **ARE** used in disciform (stromal immune) keratitis – always combined with antiviral cover\". The word that decides it is **epithelial**. Epithelial disease is virus doing damage — suppress the virus. Stromal disciform disease is the *immune response* doing the damage — suppress the response, under antiviral cover.\n\n⚠️ **This is the antecedent of `opmcq-c8-23`.** Its key \"Answer: B\" is orphaned onto the head of p.60.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain asks the same prohibition from the consequence end — `opqb-t8-301` keys **corneal perforation** as what is feared from topical steroids in a viral corneal ulcer, and `opqb-t8-326` asks the identical question for a **bacterial** ulcer with the same key. This question asks what is contraindicated; those ask why.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Topical steroids are absolutely contraindicated in epithelial herpes simplex keratitis because they promote viral replication and lead to a geographic ulcer and perforation; they are permitted only in disciform stromal disease, under antiviral cover.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.59 (Ch.8 Q22; ⚠️ the key "Answer: B" is orphaned onto the head of p.60)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 29-year-old woman has eye redness, pain and blurred vision, and fluorescein staining reveals a dendritic ulcer in the affected eye. What is the best line of management?",
    options:["Ganciclovir eye drops","Surgical intervention","A & B together","Neither A nor B"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, what is the best line of management?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c8-22`, printed immediately above on book p.60, and it was read rather than assumed. The restatement carries that patient's **findings only** — it does not say that steroids are contraindicated, because that is `opmcq-c8-22`'s own key. Options C and D are printed \"C. A & B together\" and \"D. Neithe A nor B\"; the leading option letters are stripped per the transcription rule, and the internal references to A and B are part of the option text and are kept.\n\n**The deck names the drug twice, and once by this exact preparation.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Clinical Scenario 2: The Dendritic Ulcer\" — a 30-year-old with three days of mild eye pain, photophobia and redness, and a linear branching lesion with terminal buds:\n\n> Correct Answer: **Topical antiviral (aciclovir 3 % ointment 5×/day or ganciclovir gel)** – NO TOPICAL STEROIDS\n\nand the summary slide's one-line version: \"**HSV epithelial: topical aciclovir 3 %**\". The bank's scenario is the deck's scenario with the age moved by one year.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Ganciclovir eye drops** | The key. Named in the deck's own answer line |\n| **B. Surgical intervention** | Nothing about an epithelial dendrite is surgical. ⚠️ The nearest thing the deck offers is **debridement**: \"Debridement of infected epithelium (swab) can be **adjunctive**\" — a bedside wipe with a cotton bud, offered as an adjunct rather than as management, and not what a candidate reads into \"surgical intervention\". Keratoplasty enters this deck only for scarring, dystrophy, perforation and failed medical therapy |\n| **C. A & B together** | Fails with B |\n| **D. Neither A nor B** | Fails, because A is printed |\n\n**Why an antiviral and not simply time.** Epithelial HSV is active viral replication (the deck's own words), and untreated it enlarges. The antiviral shortens the episode and limits the defect; what it does not do is eradicate the virus, which stays latent in the trigeminal ganglion — hence the deck's separate line, \"**Prophylactic oral aciclovir 400 mg twice daily reduces recurrence rate by ~50 %**\", and hence recurrence being the disease's defining habit.\n\n⚠️ **Topical for HSV, systemic for zoster — and this chapter tests both directions.** `opmcq-c8-4` uses *topical ganciclovir* as its wrong answer for herpes zoster ophthalmicus, where the printed treatment is oral valaciclovir or famciclovir within 72 h. Here the same drug is the key. The discriminator is whether the disease is a corneal epithelial infection or a dermatomal one.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t8-303` asks the identical fact with the deck's other drug name — \"The effective treatment of dendritic ulcer of the cornea is:\" keying **Acyclovir ointment**, against surface anaesthesia and local or systemic corticosteroids. Same answer, different agent from the same printed pair.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Epithelial herpes simplex keratitis — the dendritic ulcer — is treated with a topical antiviral, aciclovir 3 % ointment or ganciclovir gel, with no steroid and no surgery; oral aciclovir prophylaxis halves the recurrence rate.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.60 (Ch.8 Q23; printed "In the previous case, what is the best line of management?" and refers back to Ch.8 Q22; option D printed "Neithe A nor B")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:"An 11-year-old boy with Down's syndrome shows angulation of the lower eyelid upon looking downwards. Which of the following is the name of this sign?",
    options:["Inverse Darier's sign","Munson's sign","Lid lag sign","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The deck defines the sign in the words the stem paraphrases.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Keratoconus: Diagnosis & Management\":\n\n> **Munson's sign: V-shaped deformity of lower lid on downgaze**\n\n\"Angulation of the lower eyelid upon looking downwards\" is that definition with *V-shaped deformity* replaced by *angulation*. The Down's syndrome detail is the same slide's risk-factor line — \"Risk factors: eye rubbing, atopy, **Down syndrome**, connective tissue disorders\" — and the age fits its \"Onset: **puberty**\".\n\n**The mechanism, which is what makes the sign specific.** The cone is a forward protrusion of the central and inferior cornea. On downgaze the globe rotates so the apex presses into the lower lid from behind, and the lid margin — normally a smooth arc — is tented into a V. It is a purely mechanical readout of corneal shape, which is why nothing that does not change corneal shape can produce it.\n\n| Option | What it actually is | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Inverse Darier's sign** | ⚠️ **The word Darier appears nowhere in this course.** Measured: `darier` returns **zero hits across all twenty-nine cached lecture files and all twelve cached book chapters**, case-insensitively. Darier's sign is dermatological — stroking a lesion of urticaria pigmentosa releases mast-cell mediators and it wheals; the *inverse* form is the reverse response described in some mastocytoma variants. It has no ocular use whatever. **Not taken from the course material** | A sign from another specialty, offered because it sounds eponymous |\n| **B. Munson's sign** | The key | |\n| **C. Lid lag sign** | ⚠️ **The strongest distractor in the chapter, because it is also a lid sign elicited on downgaze.** This course teaches it as **von Graefe's sign** in thyroid eye disease: `L10) orbit` slide 17 and `L27,28) Problems of External Appearance` both print the same figure caption — \"(D) right **lid lag on downgaze – von Graefe sign**\" — alongside Dalrymple's and Kocher's signs of lid retraction | **The discriminator is which lid.** Lid lag is the **UPPER** lid failing to follow the globe down, so sclera shows above the cornea. Munson's is the **LOWER** lid pushed into a V from behind. Same gaze direction, opposite lid, different mechanism — retraction versus protrusion |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails | |\n\n**Filed in `op-cornea-surg`** — *\"Cornea — ectasia, grafts and refractive surgery\"* — because the sign is a keratoconus sign, even though the finding the stem describes is an eyelid.\n\n⚠️ **This is `opmcq-c8-14` inverted.** That question gives the sign's name and asks the disease; this one gives the finding and asks the name. Neither is a duplicate of the other — they share no option and test the recall in opposite directions.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t8-313` and `opqb-t8-314` both list Munson's sign among keratoconus features, but neither describes the lid finding or asks for the eponym.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A V-shaped angulation of the LOWER lid on downgaze is Munson's sign, produced mechanically by the keratoconic cone; lid lag on downgaze is von Graefe's sign and belongs to the UPPER lid in thyroid eye disease.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.60 (Ch.8 Q24; stem printed "Down\'s sydnrome")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 33-year-old woman presented with sudden ocular pain after her 6-month-old infant struck her eye with his fingernail. Which of the following is the most appropriate line of management?",
    options:["Topical steroids","Topical antibiotics","Hospitalization","Watchful waiting"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A fingernail across the cornea is the textbook corneal abrasion, and the deck prescribes for it in one line.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Corneal Trauma: Approach & Management\":\n\n> **Corneal abrasion**: fluorescein staining delineates defect; **treat with topical antibiotic ± cycloplegic**; usually heals in **24–48 h**\n\nSudden onset, sharp mechanical trauma, immediate pain — `L37) Red Eye` gives the mechanism: \"**Disruption of the corneal epithelium results in significant pain for the patient.** This disruption can occur due to trauma, causing a corneal abrasion or foreign body injury.\"\n\n**Why an antibiotic for a wound that is not infected.** It is prophylaxis, not treatment. The epithelium is the cornea's barrier — \"tight junctions → **barrier function**\" — and while it is breached the stroma is exposed to whatever is in the tear film. The same deck's risk-factor list puts **ocular trauma** first among the causes of bacterial keratitis. The abrasion heals in 24–48 hours; the antibiotic covers that window.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Topical steroids** | ⚠️ The deck's flagged prescribing error, twice over: steroids \"can potentiate infection\" and \"delay healing\". On a fresh epithelial defect they slow the one process that ends the problem |\n| **B. Topical antibiotics** | The key |\n| **C. Hospitalization** | The deck sets an explicit admission threshold, and this case meets none of it: \"**Admit if**: central ulcer >2 mm, threat of perforation, poor compliance, or no improvement in 48 h\". A simple abrasion is an outpatient problem |\n| **D. Watchful waiting** | ⚠️ **Nearly defensible and worth arguing with.** The lesion is self-limiting and heals on its own in 24–48 h, so the *natural history* favours doing nothing. What defeats it is that the printed management is not \"nothing\" — the deck writes a prescription — and that the risk being managed is secondary infection, which watchful waiting does not address |\n\n⚠️ **What the deck forbids in this exact situation, and it is not on the menu.** \"NEVER prescribe topical anaesthetics for outpatient analgesia\" — they inhibit epithelial healing, cause stromal melting and end in anaesthetic keratopathy and perforation. The abraded cornea hurts enough that a patient will ask, which is why the deck flags it as one of its four common prescribing errors. Grade Gain keys precisely this at `opqb-t8-348`, where a welder with punctate erosions must **not** be given topical anaesthetic drops.\n\n⚠️ **The cycloplegic is the half the bank left out.** \"± cycloplegic\" in the deck's line, and \"Cycloplegic drops: **reduce pain from ciliary spasm**, prevent synechiae\" on the bacterial-keratitis treatment slide. It is the analgesic that is safe here, in place of the anaesthetic that is not.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A fingernail corneal abrasion is treated with a topical antibiotic, with a cycloplegic for the ciliary spasm, and heals in 24-48 hours; steroids delay healing, topical anaesthetics are forbidden, and admission is reserved for a central ulcer over 2 mm or threatened perforation.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.60 (Ch.8 Q25; stem printed "her 6-month-old infant stroke her eye")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A patient with a history of herpes simplex keratitis was mistakenly treated with topical corticosteroids. The corneal epithelial lesion enlarged and developed irregular, scalloped borders. What is this type of corneal ulcer called?",
    options:["Dendritic ulcer","Geographical ulcer","Punctate epithelial keratitis","Neurotrophic ulcer"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The deck states the entity and the deck states the cause, on two different slides.** `L15,16) Cornea`:\n\n> Epithelial (Dendritic / Geographic) — Active virus replication in epithelium · Classic dendritic ulcer with terminal buds · Fluorescein: stains the ulcer bed · **Geographic = large irregular lesion**\n>\n> Common Prescribing Errors — **Topical Steroids in Epithelial HSV**: Promotes viral replication · **Can cause geographic ulcer** · Risk of perforation\n>\n> Clinical Scenario 2 — Do NOT use steroids at this stage → **risk of geographic ulcer** → perforation\n\nThe stem is that causal chain written as a case: epithelial HSV, plus a steroid, gives a large irregular lesion. \"Scalloped borders\" is the bank's word for the deck's \"irregular\".\n\n**What has physically happened.** A dendrite is a branching *line* of dead epithelium along the path of the infected cells. Remove the immune brake with a topical steroid and the virus spreads laterally in every direction, so the branches widen and coalesce and the linear figure fills out into an amoeboid map-like patch — hence *geographic*. The terminal buds that defined the dendrite are lost as the edges merge. *(The description of how one becomes the other is **not taken from the course material**: the deck defines both entities and states the causal link, but never describes the transition.)*\n\n| Option | What it is | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Dendritic ulcer** | The starting point — \"linear branching pattern with **terminal buds** on fluorescein staining\", pathognomonic of HSV | This is what the lesion *was*. The stem says it enlarged and lost its shape |\n| **B. Geographical ulcer** | The key. \"Geographic = large irregular lesion\" | |\n| **C. Punctate epithelial keratitis** | Scattered dot-like epithelial staining — asked directly by the next question in this chapter | Multiple tiny discrete spots, not one enlarging confluent lesion |\n| **D. Neurotrophic ulcer** | \"Neurotrophic keratopathy: trigeminal anaesthesia → epithelial breakdown → persistent epithelial defect → melting\", from HSV, HZV, acoustic neuroma surgery, DM, topical anaesthetic abuse | ⚠️ **The best distractor, because HSV causes this too** — it is on the deck's own causes list. It fails on the trigger: a neurotrophic ulcer follows **loss of corneal sensation**, is typically oval with smooth rolled edges, and appears after the infection rather than during it. The stem's trigger is a steroid, not anaesthesia |\n\n⚠️ **This is the clinical consequence of `opmcq-c8-22`'s prohibition**, printed four questions later. That question asks what is contraindicated; this one shows what happens when it is given anyway. Read together they are the full pearl.\n\n⚠️ **The chapter spells the entity two ways.** Book p.60 prints \"Geographical ulcer\" here and book p.61 prints \"Geographic ulcer\" as a distractor in the very next question. Both transcribed as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A topical steroid given for epithelial herpes simplex keratitis lets the virus spread laterally, so the branching dendrite widens and coalesces into a large irregular map-like lesion — the geographic ulcer.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.60 (Ch.8 Q26)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-26b', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which term describes small, dot-like areas of epithelial staining on the cornea seen with fluorescein dye?",
    options:["Dendritic ulcer","Punctate epithelial keratitis","Geographic ulcer","Filamentary keratitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION IS PRINTED WITH NO NUMBER AT ALL.** It sits between the printed 26 and the printed 27 on book pp.60-61, in the chapter's normal typography, with its own red rules above and below. Staged and drafted as `26b`. ⚠️ Its option list also straddles the page seam: the stem plus \"A. Dendritic ulcer\" and \"B. Punctate epithelial keratitis\" close p.60; \"C. Geographic ulcer\", \"D. Filamentary keratitis\" and \"Answer: B\" open p.61. Both ends were read.\n\n**The question is a pattern-recognition question and the whole answer is the word *punctate*.** `L15,16) Cornea` names punctate epithelial keratitis twice, both times as a corneal manifestation of herpes zoster ophthalmicus (\"Corneal manifestations: **punctate epithelial keratitis**, pseudodendritic ulcer, mucous plaques, disciform keratitis, neurotrophic keratopathy\"), and the epithelium panel supplies the staining mechanism: the epithelium \"**stains with fluorescein when disrupted**\", because the tight junctions that exclude the dye are gone wherever a cell is missing.\n\n**So the pattern IS the lesion.** Fluorescein does not choose a shape; the shape is the geometry of the epithelial damage. Individual cells or tiny clusters lost across the surface give discrete dots — punctate epithelial keratitis. It is a sign of diffuse surface disease rather than of one lesion, which is why the course meets it in dry eye, in exposure, in UV injury, in toxicity and in early viral keratitis.\n\n| Option | The pattern it makes | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Dendritic ulcer** | A single branching line with terminal buds | One lesion, linear, pathognomonic of HSV — the opposite of scattered dots |\n| **B. Punctate epithelial keratitis** | Many small discrete dots | The key |\n| **C. Geographic ulcer** | One large irregular confluent patch | Asked as the key of the previous question, `opmcq-c8-26` |\n| **D. Filamentary keratitis** | Strands of mucus and degenerate epithelium adherent at one end to the cornea, staining along their length and trailing free | ⚠️ **The word appears nowhere in this course.** Measured: `filamentary` returns **exactly zero hits across all twenty-nine cached lecture files and all twelve cached book chapters**, case-insensitively. The entity is real — a feature of severe dry eye and of superior limbic keratoconjunctivitis — and the filaments are *linear and mobile*, nothing like dots. **Not taken from the course material** |\n\n⚠️ **The four options are a complete typology of fluorescein patterns, and that is what the question really tests**: dots (punctate), a line with buds (dendritic), a map (geographic), threads (filamentary). Learning them as four *shapes* is more durable than learning them as four diseases, because each shape has several causes.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t8-321` uses \"Superficial punctate keratitis\" as a **true** limb in a list of HSV presentations and keys the fungal sign as the exception, and `opqb-t8-348` has a welder whose fluorescein shows \"diffuse punctate epithelial erosions\" — the same pattern from UV injury. Neither asks for the term from its description.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Small discrete dot-like areas of fluorescein staining are punctate epithelial keratitis — the pattern of scattered individual epithelial cell loss, seen in dry eye, exposure, UV injury, toxicity and early viral keratitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.60-61 (Ch.8, ⚠️ the question is PRINTED WITH NO NUMBER, between Q26 and Q27; its option list is split across the page break, options A and B on p.60, options C and D plus the key on p.61)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:"Which corneal condition is commonly associated with chronic eye rubbing in patients with atopy?",
    options:["Band keratopathy","Keratoconus","Corneal guttata","Arcus senilis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Two of the four printed risk factors, asked together.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Keratoconus: Diagnosis & Management\":\n\n> Risk factors: **eye rubbing, atopy**, Down syndrome, connective tissue disorders\n\nand the management list ends with the deck's only imperative on the whole slide: \"**Avoid eye rubbing!**\" — the one instruction it gives a patient directly.\n\n**Why the two risk factors are really one.** Atopic disease itches, itching is rubbed, and repeated mechanical rubbing releases proteases and inflammatory mediators into the cornea while deforming it thousands of times. The stroma thins where it is worked, and a thinner cornea bulges under the same intraocular pressure. That is why the deck's exclamation mark matters: eye rubbing is the only keratoconus risk factor a patient can modify. *(The mechanistic account is **not taken from the course material**; the deck lists the risk factors and issues the instruction without linking them.)*\n\n| Option | What it is | Its actual cause |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Band keratopathy** | Calcium in Bowman's layer in the interpalpebral zone | Chronic anterior uveitis · hypercalcaemia · silicone oil in the AC · end-stage ocular disease · gout · familial · idiopathic age-related — the deck's own seven-item box. **Rubbing is not on it.** Asked at `opmcq-c8-214` and `opmcq-c8-28` |\n| **B. Keratoconus** | Progressive bilateral corneal thinning and protrusion, onset at puberty | The key |\n| **C. Corneal guttata** | \"Collagen deposited on Descemet's membrane → **guttata** (excrescences)\", progressing to a 'beaten metal' endothelium | **Fuchs' endothelial dystrophy** — heritable, female sex, 5th–6th decade, worse vision in the morning. Nothing mechanical |\n| **D. Arcus senilis** | Lipid arc at the limbus | Age; and under 45, hyperlipidaemia. Asked at `opmcq-c8-20` and `opmcq-c8-21` |\n\n**The four options are the chapter's four non-infectious corneal conditions**, one per mechanism: mechanical/ectatic, mineral, dystrophic and lipid. Only one of them has a behaviour in its causal chain.\n\n⚠️ **What follows from getting this right clinically.** Keratoconus is the one condition here with a treatment that changes its course rather than its symptoms: \"**Corneal collagen cross-linking (CXL)** – stabilize progression with UV-A + riboflavin\", indicated for \"documented **progressive** keratoconus in young patients\", and the deck is emphatic that \"CXL does **NOT** improve vision – it **stabilizes** progression\". Stopping the rubbing and cross-linking the cornea are the two interventions aimed at the disease itself.\n\n**Filed in `op-cornea-surg`** — *\"Cornea — ectasia, grafts and refractive surgery\"* — with `opmcq-c8-6`, `opmcq-c8-14`, `opmcq-c8-15` and `opmcq-c8-24`.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t8-313` puts \"Frequent vigorous eye rubbing is a risk factor\" and \"More in patients with atopy\" as two **true** limbs of a keratoconus EXCEPT question keyed on the Kayser-Fleischer ring. This question makes the same two facts the stem instead of the options.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Chronic eye rubbing and atopy are two of the four printed risk factors for keratoconus, and rubbing is the only one the patient can change — hence the deck's single imperative, avoid eye rubbing.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.61 (Ch.8 Q27)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c8-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"A 58-year-old man with a long history of chronic uveitis presents complaining of gradual, painless blurring of vision and glare. Slit-lamp examination shows a horizontal, interpalpebral, chalk-white, plaque-like opacity at the level of Bowman's layer with small clear holes around corneal nerves. The opacity has a Swiss-cheese appearance and does not stain with fluorescein. Visual axis is partially involved. The ophthalmologist decides to treat by removing the epithelium and applying a calcium-binding agent to dissolve the deposits. What is the most appropriate management?",
    options:["Phototherapeutic keratectomy (PTK)","EDTA chelation therapy","Amniotic membrane grafting","Lamellar keratoplasty"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THE STEM ANSWERS ITSELF IN ITS LAST CLAUSE, AND THAT IS AS PRINTED.** \"The ophthalmologist decides to treat by **removing the epithelium and applying a calcium-binding agent to dissolve the deposits**\" is a description of EDTA chelation with the drug's name removed. The stem is transcribed as printed, not reconstructed — this is the bank's own construction, and it is recorded rather than repaired.\n\n**The diagnosis and its treatment are one line apart in the deck.** `L15,16) Cornea`, \"Corneal Degenerations\":\n\n> **Band keratopathy: calcium deposits in Bowman's layer in the interpalpebral zone**; associated with chronic ocular disease, hypercalcaemia, **chronic uveitis**\n>\n> **Treatment of band keratopathy: EDTA chelation (effective) or excimer laser PTK**\n\nand `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 supplies the aetiology from the other side, listing \"**Calcific band keratopathy** (if the iritis becomes chronic)\" among the complications of anterior uveitis, \"especially in juvenile idiopathic arthritis\".\n\n**Every finding in the stem is diagnostic and each one excludes something.**\n\n| Finding | What it establishes |\n|---|---|\n| Long history of chronic uveitis | The deck's first-listed cause, and the book's complication |\n| **Interpalpebral**, horizontal | The exposed strip where tear evaporation concentrates calcium |\n| **Chalk-white**, at the level of Bowman's | The deposit and its plane, verbatim from the deck |\n| Small clear holes around corneal nerves → **Swiss-cheese** | The nerves piercing Bowman's leave calcium-free gaps. A pathognomonic descriptor |\n| **Does not stain** with fluorescein | The epithelium over it is intact — this is a deposit, not an ulcer |\n| Gradual, **painless**, with glare | Excludes every infective and inflammatory corneal diagnosis |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Phototherapeutic keratectomy (PTK)** | ⚠️ **ALSO A CORRECT TREATMENT, AND THIS IS THE DEFECT SHAPE THIS BANK HAS PRODUCED BEFORE** (see `opqb-t3-132`). The deck names \"excimer laser **PTK**\" in the same sentence as EDTA. It is not the key only because the stem *describes* the other one: PTK ablates tissue with a laser, it does not remove the epithelium and apply a calcium-binding agent. In practice PTK is reserved for dense or recurrent bands, or where chelation leaves an irregular surface. **The key is not disputed** |\n| **B. EDTA chelation therapy** | The key. **EDTA** — ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid — is the calcium-binding agent: the epithelium is scraped, EDTA is applied on a sponge or in a well, it chelates the calcium out of Bowman's layer, and the surface re-epithelialises. The deck calls it \"**effective**\" |\n| **C. Amniotic membrane grafting** | A surface reconstruction for persistent epithelial defects and ocular surface failure. Named nowhere in this course — `amniotic` returns only `L6) Conjunctiva`'s own not-covered record (\"amniotic … all = 0 as the deck's own text\"). It would cover the deposit, not remove it. **Not taken from the course material** |\n| **D. Lamellar keratoplasty** | Replacing anterior corneal tissue with donor cornea. Vastly out of proportion: the deck's keratoplasty indications are \"Optical: keratoconus, scarring, dystrophies, pseudophakic bullous keratopathy · Tectonic: descemetocele, perforation · Therapeutic: active keratitis failing medical therapy\". A superficial calcific plaque that a chelating agent dissolves is none of those |\n\n⚠️ **The uveitis is the reason this eye got here, and it is the thing that will bring it back.** Removing the calcium does not treat the iritis, and the deck's causes box is headed by \"**Chronic anterior uveitis**\". *(Secondary chapter: `op-uvea` — the aetiology is chronic anterior uveitis and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 lists band keratopathy among its complications. Filed primary in `op-cornea` because what the question tests is the corneal lesion and its treatment.)*\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain has the same entity split into two single-fact questions — `opqb-t8-297` (deposition of **calcium salt**) and `opqb-t8-322` (seen in the **interpalpebral** part of the cornea) — and asks its treatment nowhere. `EDTA` and `chelation` each return **zero** hits across the whole ophthalmology corpus before this entry.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Calcific band keratopathy from chronic uveitis — an interpalpebral chalk-white plaque in Bowman's layer with Swiss-cheese holes where the corneal nerves pierce it — is treated by scraping the epithelium and chelating the calcium with EDTA; excimer PTK is the deck's stated alternative.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.61 (Ch.8 Q28, the last question of the chapter)' }
